From 7b1df010c3b7daf9ca2748b22588cd525755d9d8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 17:24:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] feat: trajectory coverage for every agent, skill hashing,
 cost tracking
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Handoff §10 items 4 (partly), 5 and the trajectory gap.

**expected.json now covers all four agents.** Three of seven runs used to skip
the trajectory check, including both `connectwise-operations-agent` runs — the
agent that writes to the system of record. Trajectory coverage goes 4/4 -> 7/7.

These are baseline placeholders, not reviewer-approved ground truth, and say
so in the file: no human has confirmed those runs were correct. Replace them
from Dataverse AI Review once approved runs accumulate — "approved, no
override" is a gold trajectory by definition.

They already earn their keep. The ops expectation

    load_skill -> cw_get_ticket -> cw_update_ticket -> cw_create

catches a known-bad run that updated a ticket it had never read.

**Skills in force, by content hash.** `load_skill` returns raw markdown with
`name` and `description` frontmatter and NO version, so a content hash is the
only identity a historical run has. Each run now records
{skill_name, sha256, bytes, truncated}, `--skill-registry DIR` stores each
intact body once so the rules behind an old run can be read back, and the
report prints SKILL DRIFT when one name resolves to two hashes.

This corrects §7.4's premise. `load_skill` is the largest payload (25,023
chars) but it is not the one truncating — **every truncation in both frozen
sets is a `cw_query` result**, surviving past 8192 because gen_ai.* attributes
are largely exempt from the cap. Skill-by-reference is still worth doing for
version comparison and Task Adherence; the truncation fix belongs on
`cw_query`, as paging or field projection. A truncated skill is now flagged
separately anyway, because incomplete rules are worse than incomplete data.

**Cost and latency tracked, not gated.** Tokens and wall clock print on every
run and land in the JSON artifact so they trend from day one. `cost_latency`
only returns a verdict when `--max-tokens` or `--max-duration-ms` is set;
otherwise it skips and says it is tracking. Gate once normal is known. Current
baseline: a full triage is ~10 min wall clock and ~1M uncached tokens across
7 runs, with the analysis agent peaking at 327k context in a single turn.

Baselines re-frozen. Gating totals unchanged at 5/7 and 0/2.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0145AG8KerLg2fDJTVJDwy9T
