From 10fab70cc1f7aaad38ac20ff5c9c056e897f5729 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 17:24:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] feat: use Foundry for what Foundry does, and say where
 the line is
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Answers "are we hand-rolling anything Foundry offers for little effort".
docs/FOUNDRY.md is the durable version; the short answer is three surfaces.

  deterministic checks   process quality on RECORDED production traces
                         free, no judge variance          -> merge gate
  ai-agent-evals action  agents INVOKED with a query set in staging,
                         confidence intervals + significance -> agent change
  continuous evaluation  judged metrics on sampled live traffic -> always on

Handed to Foundry:

- **§10.9 statistics.** `microsoft/ai-agent-evals` reports confidence
  intervals and tests significance against a baseline agent version. Not
  writing pass@k.
- **§5 replay harness.** The action invokes agents from a data file. With a dev
  ConnectWise instance and a Foundry staging project now available, the most
  expensive open item in the handoff costs no code. `replay/full-triage.json`
  plus `.github/workflows/staging-replay.yml`, fired by repository_dispatch
  from the agents repo. The ticket ids are placeholders.
- **§10.7 judged evaluators and portal history.** `submit_to_foundry.py` maps
  eval_runs.jsonl to the evaluator schema (`actions` -> `tool_calls`), samples
  because judging costs inference per row, and uploads. `--dry-run` converts
  with no SDK and no spend.

Kept ours, with a reason:

- The ConnectWise failure taxonomy — no Foundry equivalent.
- `valid_tool_args` as the gate. **ToolCallAccuracyEvaluator returns _pass_ for
  tool types it does not support**, with a reason string saying so. If the MCP
  toolbox tools do not register as Function Tools it reports green without
  evaluating. A pass meaning "did not evaluate" is worse than no check, so the
  judged one is a second opinion, never the gate.

Also `fetch_tool_manifest.py`: calls `tools/list` on the toolbox MCP endpoint
— the URL is already the span name of every MCP call in our traces — and
writes a manifest. Stdlib only, DefaultAzureCredential or a pasted token.
Before using it, try `AIAgentConverter`, which returns `tool_definitions` read
from the Agent Service rather than telemetry and may close §6 outright; the
thread id is in our traces as `gen_ai.conversation.id`.

The staging workflow refuses to run unless the project endpoint looks like
staging. It is the one job that invokes agents, and the ops agent writes to a
system of record.

